WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.046
Cloud incident response is the organized process for detecting, analyzing, containing, recovering from, and learning from security incidents in cloud environments.

2
00:00:11.046 --> 00:00:20.238
It uses the same discipline as traditional incident response, but the evidence, control points, and recovery actions often look different.

3
00:00:20.238 --> 00:00:35.145
A responder may need to understand identities, policies, API activity, storage permissions, network controls, serverless functions, managed services, and SaaS integrations rather than only host images and network packets.

4
00:00:35.145 --> 00:00:40.629
Traditional incident response often starts with a compromised endpoint or server.

5
00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:52.525
Cloud incidents may start with a suspicious login, a new access key, an unexpected policy change, an exposed storage bucket, abnormal data transfer, or automated resource creation.

6
00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:58.318
The control plane matters because many important actions happen through provider APIs.

7
00:00:58.318 --> 00:01:07.819
A single identity with excessive permissions can change infrastructure, read data, create resources, and alter logging if safeguards are weak.

8
00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:11.063
Cloud environments are also highly distributed.

9
00:01:11.063 --> 00:01:23.190
A single business application may use several accounts, subscriptions, projects, regions, storage services, queues, databases, identity providers, and third-party integrations.

10
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:29.833
Incident responders need to scope the incident across those boundaries without making assumptions.

11
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:35.317
The first alert may be only one symptom of a broader identity or configuration issue.

12
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:40.183
Good response work is authorized, documented, and evidence-preserving.

13
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:48.680
Responders should know who approved the work, what systems are in scope, what actions were taken, and what evidence was collected.

14
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.461
The goal is not only to stop immediate harm.

15
00:01:51.461 --> 00:02:03.202
The goal is to understand what happened, protect affected data and systems, restore trust, and support legal, privacy, compliance, and business decisions with defensible facts.

16
00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:07.836
Cloud incident response is therefore both technical and operational.

17
00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:15.174
It requires preparation, visibility, clear roles, careful containment, communication, and follow-through.

18
00:02:15.174 --> 00:02:21.199
A fast reaction that destroys evidence or causes avoidable outages can create new problems.

19
00:02:21.199 --> 00:02:24.443
A strong response balances urgency with control.
